-- Automated Texas news batch: five major stories and five viral stories selected after duplicate checks against current Keep TX Red coverage.
WITH items(slug,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,image_url,image_alt_text,body) AS (VALUES
('2026-08-09-ice-body-cameras-texas-enforcement','Border','ICE Says Every Field Officer Will Have a Body Camera by End of August After Texas Shooting Scrutiny','The agency says all field officers and agents will be equipped with body-worn cameras by month’s end, a policy shift with direct relevance in Texas after recent deadly enforcement encounters.','Associated Press','https://apnews.com/article/84a5b1eaa26a4d0e165f4a31a7ed9773','2026-08-09T13:30:00Z'::timestamptz,40,TRUE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/ice-body-cameras-texas-enforcement.svg','Editorial illustration of a federal immigration officer body camera, a Texas outline and a transparency document.','U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement says every field officer and agent will be equipped with a body-worn camera by the end of August, a significant operational change that has immediate relevance in Texas after recent immigration-enforcement shootings drew questions about transparency and evidence. The Associated Press reported Sunday that ICE expects the rollout to cover its field force nationwide, expanding a technology that many local police departments already treat as routine. The change comes as federal immigration operations remain highly visible across Texas and as local officials in Houston continue to examine the fatal shooting of Lorenzo Salgado Araujo. That case became especially contentious because there was no body-camera recording of the encounter available to settle competing accounts of what happened. ICE’s new equipment commitment does not automatically guarantee that video will be released quickly after future incidents. Agency leadership retains discretion to delay or withhold footage when officials believe release could interfere with an investigation, expose sensitive information or conflict with federal law and privacy rules. That means the policy creates a new evidence source without fully resolving the separate debate over public access. For Texas residents, prosecutors and local governments, the practical difference could still be substantial. Body cameras can preserve conversations, commands, timing, officer positioning and other details that become difficult to reconstruct after a fast-moving encounter. They can also protect officers when accusations are inconsistent with recorded evidence. The technology is not perfect; cameras can be blocked, turned away, activated late or fail to capture what an officer perceived outside the lens. Even so, recorded video frequently becomes one of the most important pieces of evidence after a use-of-force event. ICE says the deployment is part of a broader effort to improve accountability and documentation. The real test will be how consistently cameras are activated and how the agency handles requests for footage when incidents attract public scrutiny. In Texas, where immigration enforcement is both frequent and politically charged, those implementation details will matter as much as the hardware itself. The change should make future investigations more evidence-rich, but it will not end disagreements over federal enforcement policy or the public’s right to see what federal officers record.'),
('2026-08-09-txdot-loop410-us281-rebuild','Texas','TxDOT Starts $156 Million Loop 410–U.S. 281 Interchange Rebuild in San Antonio','The multiyear project will realign ramps, frontage roads and intersections at one of San Antonio’s busiest highway junctions, with construction expected through 2029.','San Antonio Express-News','https://www.expressnews.com/news/article/txdot-281-loop-410-project-ramps-22375343.php','2026-08-09T13:31:00Z'::timestamptz,38,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/txdot-loop410-us281-rebuild.svg','Editorial illustration of highway ramps crossing above San Antonio with construction barriers and a TxDOT-style road map.','Drivers in San Antonio are entering a multiyear construction cycle at one of the city’s busiest highway junctions as the Texas Department of Transportation begins a $156 million overhaul around Loop 410 and U.S. 281. The project focuses primarily on ramps, frontage roads and connecting intersections rather than rebuilding the main highway lanes themselves. That distinction matters because the goal is to improve how traffic enters, exits and moves through the interchange, where congestion and crash risk can build even when through lanes remain open. Plans include ramp realignments designed to improve sight distance, additional turn capacity near San Pedro Avenue and a continuous green-T style intersection at Northwest Military Highway and the eastbound Loop 410 frontage road. TxDOT has been studying the area for years, reflecting the combination of high traffic volumes, nearby commercial development and complicated merging patterns. Construction has been awarded to Webber and is expected to continue through the end of 2029. The work will create recurring closures and lane shifts, so the most immediate effect for drivers will be disruption before any long-term benefit arrives. Transportation planners say the redesigned system could substantially reduce crashes and shorten future travel times as the region continues to grow. The interchange serves commuters, shoppers, airport traffic and businesses across north-central San Antonio, which makes even small bottlenecks ripple into surrounding streets. For residents, the useful question is not whether construction will be inconvenient; it will. The bigger question is whether the finished layout prevents the recurring merge conflicts and queue backups that make the interchange frustrating today. TxDOT says it intends to provide advance notice of closures and major traffic changes as work progresses. Businesses near San Pedro, the airport corridor and Loop 410 will also be watching access carefully because prolonged construction can shift customer patterns. The project is another example of the costly infrastructure pressure created by rapid population and job growth in Texas metro areas. San Antonio has spent years widening corridors and rebuilding interchanges, but growth often arrives faster than major road projects can be designed and completed. This one will be judged over several years, first by how well TxDOT manages construction and later by whether the new ramp system actually makes one of the city’s most difficult traffic areas safer and easier to navigate.'),
('2026-08-09-sb37-texas-university-oversight','Education','Texas Universities Move Deeper Into SB 37 Reviews as Regents Gain More Control Over Core Curriculum','Public university systems are implementing a state law that requires recurring curriculum reviews and gives governing boards broader oversight of academic programs and faculty governance.','Texas Tribune','https://www.texastribune.org/2026/07/28/university-texas-law-scrutiny-update-testimony-censorship-2/','2026-08-09T13:32:00Z'::timestamptz,39,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/sb37-texas-university-oversight.svg','Editorial illustration of a Texas university building, curriculum papers and a governing board reviewing course lists.','Texas public universities are moving deeper into implementation of Senate Bill 37, a sweeping higher-education governance law that shifts more authority over curriculum and university decision-making toward governor-appointed governing boards. The law requires regents to conduct recurring reviews of general education curriculum, gives boards broader influence over academic programs and faculty governance, and creates new oversight mechanisms through the Texas Higher Education Coordinating Board. University leaders have told lawmakers that implementation is underway, while faculty members and students have argued that the new structure can encourage self-censorship and weaken academic independence. The disagreement is likely to intensify as the first major statutory deadlines approach. Under the enrolled law, governing boards must complete their initial general-education curriculum review and certify compliance by January 1, 2027. The review is supposed to examine whether required courses are foundational, useful for civic and professional life, connected to workforce preparation and consistent with accreditation requirements. Boards must also consider whether curriculum choices add unnecessary tuition costs or time to graduation. Supporters say the changes restore accountability to publicly funded institutions and ensure elected state leaders, through appointed regents, have a stronger line of responsibility for what universities require students to study. Critics argue that boards are not academic bodies and that political pressure could influence decisions about legitimate teaching and research. The Texas Tribune has documented faculty and student concerns that some institutions are already narrowing course offerings or changing classroom practices. State senators, however, have praised universities for carrying out the law and have signaled interest in strengthening oversight rather than reversing it. For Texas families, the effects may eventually appear in core course requirements, availability of certain minors or certificates, faculty-senate influence and the kinds of programs universities choose to maintain. The law also requires periodic review of low-enrollment minor degree and certificate programs, creating pressure to consolidate offerings that lack clear student demand or workforce justification. This is not a one-semester policy change. SB 37 builds a recurring governance system that will shape public higher education for years. The key measure will be whether the new oversight produces clearer, less costly academic pathways without narrowing legitimate scholarship or politicizing routine curriculum decisions. Texans should watch the January compliance reports closely because they will provide the first broad evidence of how far governing boards are willing to use their new authority.'),
('2026-08-09-kerr-courthouse-flood-mold-cleanup','Texas','Kerr County Courthouse Cleanup Turns to Mold Prevention After Severe Flood Damage','The courthouse remains disrupted after floodwater damaged its lower level, forcing offices to relocate while crews race to dry the historic facility safely.','KSAT / Kerr County','https://www.ksat.com/topic/July_2026_Floods/','2026-08-09T13:33:00Z'::timestamptz,37,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/kerr-courthouse-flood-mold-cleanup.svg','Editorial illustration of the Kerr County Courthouse with drying fans, water lines and flood cleanup equipment.','Recovery from the July Hill Country floods has moved inside the Kerr County Courthouse, where crews are now focused on drying the building and preventing mold after floodwater caused serious damage to the lower level. Kerr County previously announced that the courthouse would be closed to the public for an undetermined period and that offices would be relocated while the facility is assessed and repaired. The cleanup is more than a cosmetic job. Once floodwater enters walls, flooring, insulation and mechanical spaces, moisture can remain trapped long after standing water disappears. Mold can begin growing quickly in warm Texas conditions, creating health concerns and potentially turning a repair project into a much more expensive remediation effort. KSAT reported that officials are urging residents and property owners dealing with similar flood damage to use qualified remediation professionals rather than assuming a building is safe once surfaces appear dry. The courthouse closure is one visible piece of a much larger recovery effort across Kerr County. July flooding damaged roads, bridges, homes and public facilities across the region. County officials have said several bridges sustained major damage, while hundreds of homes were affected. The courthouse is especially important because it houses functions residents rely on for records, courts, county administration and other public services. Relocating offices can keep government operating, but it also complicates access for residents who may already be dealing with insurance claims, damaged property and disrupted transportation. The cleanup will likely involve moisture testing, removal of damaged materials, air handling and repeated inspections before affected spaces can safely reopen. Historic public buildings can be more difficult to repair because older construction methods and materials do not always respond to water intrusion the same way newer facilities do. For Kerr County taxpayers, the final cost is still developing. Insurance, disaster assistance and local funds may all play roles in restoration. The immediate priority is preventing additional damage while preserving essential operations. The courthouse story also offers a practical warning for homeowners throughout flood-affected Texas: visible water is only the first stage of the problem. Drying, sanitation and mold prevention are what determine whether a building can return to normal without creating a second health and structural crisis weeks later.'),
('2026-08-09-canyon-lake-full-capacity-recovery','Texas','Canyon Lake Reaches Full Conservation Capacity After Remarkable One-Month Rebound','The reservoir rose from roughly 61% full in early July to 100% of conservation capacity in early August, a dramatic recovery after years of drought pressure.','MySA','https://www.mysanantonio.com/lifestyle/outdoors/article/canyon-lake-water-levels-22374263.php','2026-08-09T13:34:00Z'::timestamptz,36,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/canyon-lake-full-capacity-recovery.svg','Editorial illustration of Canyon Lake filling between limestone hills with a reservoir gauge rising to 100 percent.','Canyon Lake has reached 100 percent of its conservation capacity for the first time in nearly five years, completing one of the most dramatic reservoir rebounds in Texas this summer. Water data cited by MySA showed the lake at about 910.17 feet above mean sea level on August 7, holding roughly 388,568 acre-feet of water. Only a month earlier, the reservoir was around 61 percent full. Heavy July rainfall across the Hill Country sent massive inflows into rivers and reservoirs, creating destructive flooding in some communities while also reversing years of drought-related water loss. Canyon Lake gained roughly 157,000 acre-feet in a month, a change large enough to be visible in shoreline conditions, marina access and satellite imagery. The reservoir’s conservation pool is around 909 feet, so current levels are slightly above that target while still far below the much higher flood-control pool. Operators are balancing inflows and controlled releases as the system returns to more normal conditions. The recovery matters well beyond recreation. Canyon Lake is part of the region’s water-supply system and has been closely watched during prolonged drought as falling levels exposed shoreline, affected boat access and raised concerns about long-term supply reliability. A full conservation pool gives the region breathing room, but it does not erase those concerns permanently. Reservoir levels can decline rapidly during extended heat and dry weather, especially as municipal demand rises and evaporation accelerates. The turnaround also carries a difficult contrast. The same rain that restored the lake contributed to damaging floods elsewhere in Central and South Texas. Water managers therefore have to view a full reservoir as both a resource and a system that must preserve space for future flood control. For residents who have watched Canyon Lake retreat year after year, the visual change is striking. Areas that looked unusually dry last summer are again underwater, boat ramps are in better shape and the lake has returned to a level many longtime visitors recognize. The broader lesson is how quickly Texas water conditions can swing between scarcity and excess. One wet month can dramatically improve a reservoir, but lasting water security still depends on conservation, infrastructure and planning for the next drought rather than assuming the current abundance will continue.'),
('2026-08-09-san-antonio-dallas-moving-trends','Viral','Dallas Emerges as the Top Texas Destination for San Antonio Movers Across Generations','A U-Haul analysis of one-way moves found Dallas leading net destination gains from the San Antonio-New Braunfels area across age groups.','MySA','https://www.mysanantonio.com/news/local/article/san-antonio-dallas-moves-22367552.php','2026-08-09T13:35:00Z'::timestamptz,29,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/san-antonio-dallas-moving-trends.svg','Editorial illustration of a moving truck traveling from San Antonio to Dallas with Texas highway signs and generational icons.','A new look at one-way moving activity is putting Dallas at the top of the list for people leaving the San Antonio-New Braunfels area, and the pattern appears across multiple generations. MySA reported on U-Haul data covering one-way truck and trailer rentals from July 2025 through June 2026. The analysis found Dallas producing the strongest net gains from San Antonio-area moves when all age groups were considered together. The details vary by generation, which is what makes the data more interesting than a simple city ranking. Gen X movers showed strong interest in Dallas, Houston and College Station. Millennials frequently chose Austin, Houston and major out-of-state metros, while Gen Z also leaned toward Dallas, Austin and Houston among Texas destinations. Boomers showed a somewhat different pattern, with several out-of-state destinations ranking highly. Rental data is not the same thing as a complete census migration report. It captures customers using a specific moving company and does not measure every household move, so it should be treated as a directional signal rather than a precise population count. Still, one-way truck rentals are a useful real-time indicator because they reflect actual relocation behavior before official population estimates are released. The Dallas result reinforces a broader Texas trend: the state’s major metros are increasingly exchanging residents with one another, not simply attracting newcomers from California, New York or other states. Jobs, housing costs, family ties and lifestyle differences can all push Texans from one metro to another. Dallas-Fort Worth offers a large and diverse employment base, while San Antonio remains attractive for military, health care, tourism and comparatively lower housing costs. That means movement can occur in both directions even when one metro posts a net advantage in a particular dataset. The viral appeal of the report comes from the generational breakdown. Texans often assume younger movers want Austin while older movers favor smaller or cheaper communities, but the data shows more overlap than stereotypes suggest. Dallas appears repeatedly because its labor market and scale appeal to a wide range of households. For San Antonio leaders, the practical question is whether movers are leaving for better jobs, different housing options or other quality-of-life reasons that local policy can influence. For everyone else, it is another reminder that Texas migration is increasingly an internal reshuffling among fast-growing metros.'),
('2026-08-09-san-antonio-pools-weekends-labor-day','Viral','Seven San Antonio Pools Will Stay Open on Weekends After Most City Pools Close','Most seasonal city pools are ending daily operations, but seven locations will remain open Saturdays and Sundays through Labor Day.','San Antonio Express-News','https://www.expressnews.com/lifestyle/article/san-antonio-public-pools-free-open-labor-day-22377878.php','2026-08-09T13:36:00Z'::timestamptz,26,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/san-antonio-pools-weekends-labor-day.svg','Editorial illustration of a San Antonio public pool, weekend calendar and families swimming under late-summer sun.','San Antonio’s summer pool season is beginning to wind down, but residents will still have several free options for cooling off as August heat continues. Most city-operated public pools are closing their regular seasonal schedules, while seven locations will remain open on Saturdays and Sundays through Labor Day. The extended schedule matters because South Texas is heading into another hot stretch, with afternoon temperatures forecast to climb toward the upper 90s and around 100 degrees. The seven pools continuing weekend operations are scheduled to open from 1 p.m. to 8 p.m., giving families a public alternative to staying indoors during the hottest part of late summer. Woodlawn Lake Pool will also continue limited weekday programming, including lap swimming and aqua fitness during morning hours. San Antonio’s splash pads remain another option and are scheduled to operate daily well beyond the end of the traditional pool season. The city typically adjusts aquatic staffing and hours once schools restart, which is why many pools close even though Texas summer temperatures remain high. Lifeguard availability, seasonal staffing and school schedules all influence how long full operations can continue. Extending weekend hours at selected sites concentrates staff while keeping access available in multiple parts of the city. For parents, the change is worth checking before leaving home because a pool that was open last weekend may no longer be operating on the same schedule. The city’s parks department posts current locations and hours, and weather or maintenance issues can still cause temporary closures. The extended access also has a public-health benefit. During periods of high heat, public pools, libraries and cooling centers can give families without reliable air conditioning safer places to spend part of the day. Swimming comes with its own risks, so children should still be closely supervised even when lifeguards are present. The story is spreading because it answers a very practical late-summer question: where can families still swim for free once the regular season ends? In San Antonio, the answer is not every neighborhood pool, but it is more than none. Seven city pools will carry the season through Labor Day weekends while splash pads help fill the gap across the rest of the city.'),
('2026-08-09-san-antonio-electric-cart-crash','Viral','Man Riding an Electric Shopping Cart Is Hospitalized After San Antonio Collision','Police say a man using a motorized shopping cart was struck by a vehicle on the West Side, an unusual crash that quickly drew attention online.','KSAT','https://www.ksat.com/','2026-08-09T13:37:00Z'::timestamptz,27,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/san-antonio-electric-cart-crash.svg','Editorial illustration of an electric shopping cart near a San Antonio roadway with emergency lights and a pedestrian safety warning.','An unusual San Antonio traffic case is drawing attention after police said a man riding a motorized shopping cart was struck by a vehicle and taken to a hospital. KSAT listed the incident among its most-read local stories Sunday morning. The circumstances stand out because powered shopping carts are designed for low-speed use inside and immediately around stores, not as road vehicles. When one enters a traffic lane or crosses a street, the rider has little physical protection and may be difficult for drivers to see, particularly at night or in areas without dedicated pedestrian crossings. San Antonio police have not released every detail surrounding the collision, and any determination of fault will depend on the investigation. The case nevertheless highlights a recurring transportation problem in growing Texas cities: streets often have to serve drivers, pedestrians, wheelchair users, cyclists and people relying on unconventional mobility devices at the same time. Road designs built primarily for cars can leave vulnerable users with few safe options. Electric shopping carts are especially challenging because they move faster than walking speed but generally lack lights, reflectors, turn signals and other equipment expected on street-legal vehicles. For drivers, the practical lesson is to expect slow-moving and vulnerable road users near shopping centers, apartment complexes and transit stops, especially in areas where sidewalks are incomplete. For riders, leaving a commercial property in a store mobility cart can create serious danger even for a short trip. The viral nature of the incident should not obscure the severity of the outcome: a person was hospitalized after being hit by a vehicle. San Antonio has invested in pedestrian-safety programs and street redesigns, but the city continues to face difficult tradeoffs on wide arterial roads where vehicle speeds are high and safe crossings may be spaced far apart. Police will determine what happened in this specific case. The broader issue is already clear. A low-speed mobility device offers almost no protection when it meets a full-size vehicle, and the margin for driver error is tiny. The incident is a reminder that traffic safety depends not only on conventional cars and trucks but also on recognizing the growing variety of people and devices sharing urban streets.'),
('2026-08-09-midland-county-judge-removal-dispute','Viral','Midland County Judge Faces Escalating Removal Fight After Privacy Lawsuit and DA Action','A dispute over a social-media post, alleged disclosure of personal information and a separate removal proceeding is intensifying around County Judge Terry Johnson.','Midland Reporter-Telegram','https://www.mrt.com/news/article/midland-tx-da-county-judge-conference-22377179.php','2026-08-09T13:38:00Z'::timestamptz,28,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/midland-county-judge-removal-dispute.svg','Editorial illustration of a West Texas courthouse, a social media post and legal documents marked removal proceeding.','A political and legal dispute in Midland County is escalating around County Judge Terry Johnson, with a federal privacy-related lawsuit, public calls for his resignation and a separate removal proceeding now converging. The Midland Reporter-Telegram reported that residents Nino Rodriquez, also known as Nino America, and Midland ISD School Board President Brandon Hodges are pursuing pre-suit discovery tied to a Facebook post attributed to Johnson. Their attorney says the dispute involves alleged publication of personal information and raises questions about how government officials handle sensitive data. The controversy intensified when Midland County District Attorney Glenn Harwood announced a public briefing and a Chapter 87 removal action surfaced against Johnson. Texas law provides a court process for removing certain county officials under specified circumstances, but filing such a proceeding is not the same as a final finding that removal is warranted. Johnson has also faced public pressure at commissioners court, where one resident called for his resignation and raised broader concerns about government surveillance tools and public trust. The facts and legal claims remain contested, and allegations in civil or removal proceedings should not be treated as proven until a court rules. Even so, the conflict matters because a county judge in Texas is not simply a ceremonial figure. County judges preside over commissioners court and can play major roles in budgets, emergency management and local administration. A prolonged legal fight can therefore become an operational issue as well as a political one. Johnson previously lost his reelection bid and is expected to leave office when his term ends, but critics argue that the current dispute requires action sooner. Supporters or defenders may argue that the legal process should run its course without turning allegations into a political verdict. For Midland County residents, the case now involves three separate questions: whether personal information was improperly disclosed, whether the conduct meets the legal standard for removal, and whether public confidence has deteriorated enough to affect county governance before the term ends. The story is attracting attention because those questions are colliding in public rather than unfolding quietly in court. Texans should watch for formal filings and judicial rulings rather than relying on social-media claims, because the next meaningful developments will come from the legal record.'),
('2026-08-09-south-texas-dry-heat-110-index','Viral','South Texas Has Gone 22 of 23 Days Without Measurable Rain as Heat Index Heads Toward 110','San Antonio’s wet year has abruptly shifted into a late-summer dry spell, with triple-digit heat expected to intensify during the coming week.','San Antonio Express-News','https://www.expressnews.com/san-antonio-weather/forecast/article/weather-forecast-san-antonio-texas-rain-hot-22380119.php','2026-08-09T13:39:00Z'::timestamptz,28,FALSE,'/images/news/generated/2026-08-09/south-texas-dry-heat-110-index.svg','Editorial illustration of San Antonio under a blazing sun, a dry rain gauge and a heat index climbing toward 110 degrees.','San Antonio is entering a hotter and drier stretch after going 22 of the past 23 days without measurable rainfall, an abrupt change in a year that has otherwise been unusually wet. The San Antonio Express-News reports that the city has received about 24.79 inches of rain so far in 2026, roughly 31 percent above normal for this point in the year and the highest early-August total in more than a decade. That larger annual surplus can make the current dry spell sound less significant than it feels on the ground. Lawns, soils and vegetation can dry quickly under repeated upper-90s temperatures, and the risk of heat illness depends on current conditions rather than year-to-date rainfall. Sunday carries only a small chance of isolated showers, generally around 10 to 20 percent in the San Antonio area. After that, high pressure is expected to strengthen, pushing afternoon temperatures toward 100 degrees by midweek. Humidity could make it feel closer to 110 at times. The combination of hot days and warm nights can be particularly dangerous for older adults, outdoor workers, children and people without reliable air conditioning because the body gets less overnight relief. The forecast also matters as schools resume and fall sports practices accelerate. Coaches and marching bands increasingly rely on wet-bulb globe temperature readings rather than air temperature alone because direct sun, wind and humidity can dramatically change how strenuous outdoor activity feels. A 98-degree day can create very different risk depending on those conditions. The current pattern does not mean South Texas is back in a long-term drought after the heavy rain earlier this year. It does show how quickly summer weather can swing from flood concerns to heat and dryness. Forecast models offer little sign of widespread rain during the coming week, so residents should plan around heat rather than expecting storms to provide meaningful relief. Hydration, shade and scheduling outdoor work earlier in the day remain the simplest protections. The broader Texas lesson is familiar: annual rainfall totals can look healthy while short-term weather still creates serious local stress. San Antonio has plenty of water in the year-to-date column, but the next several days will feel much more like a traditional August.')
), prepared AS (
SELECT slug,'/news/'||slug,FALSE,category,title,dek,source_name,source_url,published_at,score,is_breaking,'news',body,
jsonb_build_object('updated','2026-08-09','intro',jsonb_build_array(dek),'sections',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('heading','The story','paragraphs',jsonb_build_array(body))),'faq','[]'::jsonb,'sources',jsonb_build_array(jsonb_build_object('label',source_name||' — source report','url',source_url)),'keyTakeaways',jsonb_build_array(dek)),image_url,image_url,image_alt_text FROM items
)
